-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Learning Objectives
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC By the end of this lesson, you will be able to:
-- MAGIC - Describe the Identity and Access Model used by Databricks
-- MAGIC - Configure IdP federation and SCIM group sync with your Databricks account

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## A. Roles in Databricks
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let’s begin with a discussion of the specialized roles that Databricks supports that convey specific privileges to principals holding this role.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A1. Cloud Administrator
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Databricks supports three different cloud compute platforms: Amazon AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform. A Databricks instance requires underlying cloud resources for storage, compute resources, and securing the platform. This cloud-specific management is the responsibility of a cloud administrator.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Cloud admins administer underlying cloud resources like:
-- MAGIC - Storage accounts/buckets
-- MAGIC - IAM role/service principals/Managed Identities
-- MAGIC - Integration of additional cloud services (data warehouses, streaming data sources, and more)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A2. Identity Administrator
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC A scalable, secure Databricks deployment relies on your identity provider to seamlessly enable authorized users in your organization to access Databricks with minimal manual intervention. Identity administrators are responsible for managing the identity provider side of things, as well as enabling its integration with Databricks.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC They administer and configure the services that:
-- MAGIC - Provision users and groups into the account
-- MAGIC - Mitigates need to manually create and manage identities

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A3. Account Administrator
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Account administrators are principals perform account-level management tasks that include:
-- MAGIC - Managing subscription and billing
-- MAGIC -Managing principals at the account level. This could either mean manually managing users and groups, or cooperating with an identity administrator to automate this management through an IdP. Account admins also have the ability to assign principals to workspaces.
-- MAGIC - They work with cloud administrators to create and manage metastores and workspaces and the underlying cloud resources they require. They also have the ability to assign metastores to workspaces.
-- MAGIC - They work with cloud administrators to integrate external storage, which can be used to physically isolate data or support external tables and volumes where needed.
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A4. Workspace Administrator
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Though the roles of account and workspace admin overlap somewhat, in general workspace administrators are charged with managing the resources and capabilities over a specific workspace. The tasks they can perform include:
-- MAGIC - Administering workspace assets and their permissions. These types of assets include compute resources (clusters, policies, SQL warehouses, Lakebase instances, jobs, folders and more).
-- MAGIC - They can define cluster policies and entitlements that enable or limit users abilities to create compute resources like clusters and SQL warehouses.
-- MAGIC - They can manage principals at the workspace level. Workspace admins can bring in existing account users or groups, or they can add new users to the system.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A5. Metastore administrators
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Metastore administrators are designated by account admins. They are key players in data governance with Unity Catalog, since they have full administrative capabilities over the metastore. Tasks they can perform include:
-- MAGIC - Creating and managing most metastore-level constructs like catalogs, connections, and credentials (to name a few).
-- MAGIC - They can also reassign or take ownership of objects in the metastore, thereby allowing them to manage permissions or delete objects.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Databricks recommends assigning this role as a group structure. This approach has two important benefits:
-- MAGIC - Simplifies the process of managing admins. Giving someone metastore admin can be done by simply adding them to a group. Correspondingly, they can be removed from the group to revoke the role.
-- MAGIC - The group privilege model also inherently avoids any individual in the organization becoming a bottleneck in the process. Anyone in the group can perform the needed function.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A6. Data Owner
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The last role we talk about, also crucially important in any data governance scheme, is the owner. Every object in a metastore has an owner who, by default, is the principal who created it.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Owners have a full implicit permissions on the objects they own, with the added ability to manage permissions, reassign ownership to someone else, or delete the object entirely.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let’s take a table as an example. A user that created the table can automatically read or modify the table data or metadata without having to have any explicit permissions to do so. They can drop the table, something that no one else can do – not even a metastore admin (unless they take ownership). The owner can also relinquish ownership to someone else, after which time they would need permissions to access the table.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC

