-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ## B. Principals
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In Databricks, we think of a principal as an entity that can be authenticated. When it comes to data and AI governance in Databricks, there are three types of principals which we will talk about now.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### B1. Users
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The first type of principal is a user. A user corresponds to an individual user of the system; that is, a person. Users are identified by an email address.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Users are created either manually by an account or workspace administrator, or automatically provisioned through an identity provider. But regardless of how they're managed, they are defined at the account level, like all principals. From there, they can be assigned to one or more workspaces, again by an account or workspace administrator.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Regular users of the system access the system by logging into one of the workspaces to which they're assigned using their email address and password, or through SSO if suitably configured. From there they’ll interact with the system through the workspace user interface. Assuming an admin has appropriately configured the workspace, users can also access Databricks remotely through a personal access token. This provides an alternate authentication mechanism for accessing Databricks. For example, you can use one to connect a BI tool to a SQL warehouse. Or you can access much of the platform functionality remotely using the Databricks CLI, SDKs, or REST APIs.
-- MAGIC
