-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### B3. Groups
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC We’ve discussed two types of individual principals: users and service principals. But when it comes to defining and implementing data and AI governance policies, we normally like to define things terms of roles, not specific individuals. If we only had principals defined in terms of users and service principals, policies would be a lot harder to articulate, and rules would be a lot more cumbersome to build and maintain.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Groups are a somewhat universal construct in any governance scheme you’ll see out in the wild, and for good reason. They allow us to gather users, service principals, and even other groups into a collective unit that can be operated on as one. All members of the group implicitly inherit any permissions granted to the group itself. Groups can also be assigned ownership of data and AI assets.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Groups are created at the account level, and are identified by a name. Groups are a permanent fixture which makes them well suited for representing roles in data and AI governance. Group *members*, on the other hand, can come and go as needed. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC For these reasons, it’s recommended to grant permissions and ownership on data and AI objects to groups rather than individuals.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Workspace-local Groups
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Note that Databricks also supports groups at the workspace level. Workspace-local groups predate Unity Catalog and we don’t recommend their use in UC-enabled workspaces since they cannot be used for data and AI access control. Typically, we want to migrate any workspace-local groups to the account level to achieve the data governance structures that were in place before the upgrade.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### B4. Recap
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Users are defined at the account level and map to the individual users of the system, Because users generally aren’t permanent, these principals tend to be dynamic and temporal in nature. Users are identified by their email address.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Service principals are another type of individual principal for use by jobs and external tools and integrations. Though they are assigned a name to make it easier to identify and manage, they are identified by a GUID that the system generates at creation time.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Groups are collections of principals aimed at simplifying the management of privileges and ownership.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Workspace-local groups predate Unity Catalog and provided grouping functionality at the workspace level. These cannot be used by Unity Catalog and must be migrated to the account level for managing data and AI assets.
-- MAGIC
