-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## C. IdP Federation 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Identity Provider (IdP) federation and SCIM group sync are foundational to centralized user and group management in a Unity Catalog (UC) enabled Databricks environment. IdP federation allows organizations to manage authentication and authorization using their enterprise identity system, while SCIM provisioning automates the synchronization of users and groups from the IdP to Databricks. This combination enables scalable, secure, and consistent access management across all workspaces.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### C1. IdP Federation in UC-Enabled Systems
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC With identity federation enabled, all user, group, and service principal management is performed at the account level and propagated to individual workspaces. This removes the need to configure identities separately for each workspace, ensuring that access rights—as defined in the IdP—are reflected systemwide. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Workspace admins select users and groups from the centralized account, and any changes in the IdP (such as user deactivation, entitlements, or group membership updates) are automatically reflected in Databricks.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### C2. SCIM Group Sync
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC SCIM (System for Cross-domain Identity Management) is an open standard that automates provisioning and deprovisioning. When enabled at the account level, SCIM connectors continuously synchronize user and group data from the IdP to Databricks. This means:
-- MAGIC - Account-level SCIM provisioning ensures all users and groups are managed centrally. Workspace assignments are made for these account-level identities, leveraging identity federation for granular access control. 
-- MAGIC - **Workspace-level SCIM is legacy and should be replaced by account-level SCIM once federation is enabled.** If workspace-level SCIM is still running, administrators are advised to disable it to avoid conflicts and ensure a single source of truth for groups and permissions. 
-- MAGIC - Group membership and entitlements assigned via the IdP will override changes made directly in the Databricks UI, ensuring tight coupling between enterprise access policies and Databricks data governance.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### C3. Operational Impact
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Leveraging account-level IdP federation delivers the following important benefits:
-- MAGIC - **Centralized Lifecycle Management:** Adding, updating, or removing users and groups in the IdP automatically updates access in all UC-enabled Databricks workspaces. 
-- MAGIC - **Entitlement Management:** If entitlements (like cluster creation permission) are provisioned via the IdP, any manual changes in Databricks will be overridden in the next sync, guaranteeing policy consistency. 
-- MAGIC - **Deprovisioning:** When a user or group is removed from SCIM in the IdP, they are deactivated from all Databricks workspaces and resources, regardless of federation status. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #1976d2; background: #e3f2fd; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
-- MAGIC   <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC     <span style="font-size: 24px;">ℹ️</span>
-- MAGIC     <div>
-- MAGIC       <strong>Migration Best Practice:</strong> If migrating from workspace-level SCIM, create centralized IdP groups covering all necessary users, configure an account-level SCIM connector, and disable all workspace-level SCIM connections.
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC   </div>
-- MAGIC </div>
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC

